#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# rationalize

#% text
# `rationalize(x)` gives the simplest rational number within the error of the inexact number `x`, when one is close enough.

#% text
# `rationalize(x, dx)` gives the simplest rational within `dx` of `x`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# "Simplest" means smallest denominator. Without `dx`, `x` stays as it is when no rational with a small denominator is within its error.

#% item
# `set_precision(x, inf)` gives the exact binary value of a machine number instead.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
rationalize(0.75)
#% output
#= 3/4

#% cell
rationalize(0.333333333333333)
#% output
#= 0.333333333333333

#% cell
rationalize(3.14159, 0.001)
#% output
#= 201/64

#% cell
rationalize(pi*1.0)
#% output
#= 3.141592653589793

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Compared with the exact binary value:

#% cell
[rationalize(0.1), set_precision(0.1, inf)]
#% output
#= [1/10, 3602879701896397/36028797018963968]

#% text
# Each element of a list:

#% cell
rationalize([0.5, 0.25, 0.2])
#% output
#= [1/2, 1/4, 1/5]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Approximations of `pi` with growing accuracy:

#% cell
table(rationalize(pi*1.0, 10.0^-k), k, [1, 2, 4, 6])
#% output
#= [16/5, 22/7, 333/106, 355/113]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `set_precision`, `continued_fraction`, `n`
